      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "chromatic-resolution-rule",
      "narrative": "For scalar convergence onto a dom7 chord, Martino prescribes targeting the tritone's two defining tones -- the 7th and 3rd of the dominant -- via chromatic resolution: 'It is just a matter of using chromatic tones to resolve the tritone (7th or 3rd) to use those scales against a 7th chord convergingly.' This is the concise rule for all scalar approach to the dominant. Chapter 10, p. 82.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
          "citation": "Chapter 10 p.82"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "ear-preparation-before-approach",
      "narrative": "Before deploying any approach scale against a dom7 or any target chord, Martino prescribes internalizing the destination tonality through cadential repetition: 'Before playing each scale in this system, you should brainwash your ear with the tonality (F major in this case) with several cadences into the key. (Ex. G-7-C7 FA) (G#-C 139 FA) I-VI-II-V-T ect.' The ear must know the destination before the approach begins. Chapter 4, p. 32.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
          "citation": "Chapter 4 p.32"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "aural-projection-while-comping",
      "narrative": "While executing an approach scale or chord targeting a dom7 resolution, Martino prescribes simultaneously projecting attention to the destination: 'When converging on a tonality project yourself sound wise to it already having your attention (sound attention) on it and on the chord or scale you are using.' Physical execution and aural anticipation of arrival must occur simultaneously. Chapter 4, p. 34.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
          "citation": "Chapter 4 p.34"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "chord-extraction-policy",
      "narrative": "Martino prescribes mining every scale for all constituent chords as independent convergent devices targeting the dom7 resolution: 'Extract all possible chords from all scales for resolution purposes.' The student is not to treat scales as melodic entities alone -- every voicing latent in the scale is a valid approach chord. Chapter 4, p. 34.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
