      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7b5",
      "function_role": "tritone-substitution",
      "narrative": "Martino prescribes dom7b5 chords -- both the V7b5 and its tritone substitute -- as the primary character-chord form for convergence. Chapter 7 demonstrates that 'Bb7b5, C7+5, C7b5, Gb7b5' are all interchangeable because they are tritone-related and resolve to the same FA7 target (see diagram p. 58). The tritone pair shared between Bb7b5 and Gb7b5 makes them fully substitutable as approach chords. Chapter 7, p. 58.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
          "citation": "Chapter 7 p.58"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7b5",
      "function_role": "character-chord-from-scale",
      "narrative": "The whole-tone scale (Scale #10) prescribes C7b5 as its primary linking chord: 'The linking chord is the C7b5 which resolves nicely into F major. Other chords which have resolving tendencies are the C+7, C69#11, C7b5.' The dom7b5 is the whole-tone scale's canonical harmonic product for cadential approach. Chapter 4, p. 41.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
