      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7b9",
      "function_role": "minor-key-tritone-governance",
      "narrative": "For convergence onto a minor chord, Martino prescribes identifying the specific tritone pair governing the relationship: 'The tritone (G-Db) has a G7 or C7b9 relationship to the F minor chord.' The C7b9 is thus the characteristic dominant approach chord for the F minor convergence, extending the dom7b9's role from major-key to minor-key contexts. Chapter 10, p. 84.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
          "citation": "Chapter 10 p.84"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7alt",
      "function_role": "character-chord-from-scale",
      "narrative": "Scale #13 prescribes the full altered dominant family -- C7b9b5, C7b9, C7b5 -- as its native character chords, resolving to the extended tonic FA679 (Chapter 7, p. 59). Scale #6 (Jazz Melodic Minor) also prescribes altered extensions including 'E+A7, Bb7+' as its characteristic chords for convergence (Chapter 7, p. 57). Altered dominants are the direct harmonic product of multiple scales in the system.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
          "citation": "Chapter 7 p.57"
        },
        {
          "source": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
          "citation": "Chapter 7 p.59"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7alt",
      "function_role": "tritone-substitution",
      "narrative": "Martino prescribes generating altered dominant variants on both the V chord and its tritone substitute as co-equal convergers: for a target of Eb, 'E7b5, E13b5, E9+11, E9, E13 may be used as chord convergers in addition to Bb7.' Altered tensions applied to the tritone-substitute dominant are fully licensed approach chords. Chapter 10, p. 80.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "pat-martino:system-of-tonal-convergence:convergence-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "system-of-tonal-convergence",
      "references": [
